WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.459
Today, we're going to be speaking with Graham
Wood, who's a journalist, writes for The Atlantic,

00:00:05.459 --> 00:00:10.280
also author of the book The Way of the Strangers.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:11.349
Really glad to have you on.

00:00:11.349 --> 00:00:12.610
I'm looking forward to this.

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:13.780
Thanks for having me, David.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.550
So let's talk a little bit about the concept
of a failed state.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:21.900
There are those who have been attributing
the term failed state to the United States

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:28.400
in the context of the failure to mount a reasonable
response to the coronaviruses pandemic, very

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:33.810
high rate of per capita cases and death others
have been talking about.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:37.590
The U.S. is a failed state in the context
of Trump's failed leadership in the U.S.,

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:42.090
increasingly being pushed aside on conversations
of climate and so many others.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:46.600
The idea of a failed state and that we had
a sitting president tried to subvert the will

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:52.990
of the people so brazenly as Donald Trump
tried to keep the White House, even despite

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:55.760
losing to Joe Biden.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.840
What's your sense of how accurate this term
is and how we should think about that?

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:05.439
Well, first of all, I think the term failed
state, as you point out, it's got a diversity

00:01:05.439 --> 00:01:06.729
of effects.

00:01:06.729 --> 00:01:11.039
It's sometimes just a state that doesn't exist,
sometimes a state that is malevolent, sometimes

00:01:11.039 --> 00:01:14.270
a state that doesn't do its job well.

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:20.140
And I think of the of the three probably think
most of of of the last one, but what with

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:21.829
touches of the other ones.

00:01:21.829 --> 00:01:28.649
So when I think of other states that have
failed, they're all, of course, horrible humanitarian

00:01:28.649 --> 00:01:29.649
disasters.

00:01:29.649 --> 00:01:33.930
Somalia probably being the most famous example
in the last few decades.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:37.729
And I don't see us moving in to that position.

00:01:37.729 --> 00:01:44.650
What I do see is enough institutions of government
that have broken down that you could be forgiven

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:53.229
for thinking that that the pressure tests
just exposed huge, huge flaws and shortcomings

00:01:53.229 --> 00:01:58.850
in government, and that the most worrying
thing, first of all, is that they exist.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:02.420
And second of all, if there seems to be no
remedy for them, they don't seem to be getting

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:03.420
any better.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:09.890
And there is no impetus, certainly with the
current administration, to do what I would

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:13.030
say the necessary work would be to correct
them.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:16.340
Yeah, a lot of it is failed.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:17.680
Failure to meet expectations.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:18.680
Right.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:23.990
The when we talk about a failed state, it's
not necessarily some of the things you mentioned,

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:28.030
but it's we might have imagined than those
who believe in American exceptionalism might

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:29.350
have imagined.

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:33.220
We would have been one of the best countries
in dealing with the pandemic, a rich country

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:34.730
with so many resources.

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:38.520
So so maybe a lot of it is about the failure
to meet expectations.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:44.350
Same applies to the Democratic thing, as we
might call it, with regard to the 2020 election.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:45.920
Just not what people expected.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:46.920
Yeah.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.020
So I think we can go beyond politics, too.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:56.281
I mean, the tools that the world has used
to handle the pandemic successfully, where

00:02:56.281 --> 00:02:59.820
it's done so successfully, we're largely American
creations.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:08.140
I mean, American ingenuity created things
like PCR, for example, that has been deployed

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:13.620
at scale with great success in smaller countries,
with fewer resources than we in the United

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:14.730
States have.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:20.460
So you start seeing the the expectations,
just not not being lived up to.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.550
That's that's, I think, the main thing.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:27.070
I mean, there's also, though, the fact that
the United States has really never stopped

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:35.520
thinking of itself as in in idealistic terms,
whereas other countries, I think, have been

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.390
more cynical about their role in the world.

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:44.530
So, you know, it's not as likely, I think,
to say someone who's from the UK thinking

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:50.510
that the UK is going to be a beacon for hope
that the model of the UK is going to be exported

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:51.510
elsewhere.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.930
As Americans, we we inherit that.

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:55.950
That's it's part of our legacy.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:04:02.070
And so we also see ourselves failing to live
up to not just our capacities, but our ideals

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:08.310
that we tell ourselves and then to a large
extent tell other people to you know, I got

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:13.330
a very interesting phone calls in the last
couple of weeks since the election from British

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:16.960
and Belgian viewers, a British and a Belgian
viewer.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:21.889
And they said, you know, David, and watching
the election take place in the run up to November

00:04:21.889 --> 00:04:29.050
3rd and what happened there and after, it's
really weird to see people talking about our

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:34.189
democracy in a campaign like a discussion
of the concept of our democracy in a campaign,

00:04:34.189 --> 00:04:40.419
our democracy being at risk or an attack on
democracy, that it was unusual to even see

00:04:40.419 --> 00:04:45.909
that discussion and it would be unusual in
places like the U.K. and Belgium and maybe

00:04:45.909 --> 00:04:46.909
others.

00:04:46.909 --> 00:04:47.909
What's your thought about that?

00:04:47.909 --> 00:04:50.289
Yeah, I think to a lot of the world, Americans
look very naive.

00:04:50.289 --> 00:04:55.080
I mean, we think that these stories we tell
ourselves are.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:05:02.139
True, we don't assume that they're offered
in purely cynical way, and I think that's

00:05:02.139 --> 00:05:06.410
a good thing about the United States and it's
something that I actually wish other countries

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:15.529
would would do a bit more of maybe with with
with the with some revisions, as I wish that

00:05:15.529 --> 00:05:16.669
we would do some revisions.

00:05:16.669 --> 00:05:22.340
You know, there's so much discussion of the
sins of America and the sins actually happened.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:25.439
So, yeah, we revise our idealism on that basis.

00:05:25.439 --> 00:05:31.580
But when a Belgian hears us talk about what
the importance is of American democracy.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:36.240
If you're a Belgian, that there is no current
strain of Belgian thought that suggests that

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:39.500
maybe the Belgian model is the savior of mankind.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:45.849
There's definitely a living version of American
exceptionalism that believes exactly that.

00:05:45.849 --> 00:05:52.020
So somewhere in the middle is probably the
right place, but it means that the failure

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:57.779
of the United States politically in public
health is all that more all that much more

00:05:57.779 --> 00:06:00.150
heartbreaking, I think, to Americans.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:08.660
What is your sense of where and how unrest
and maybe violence would would develop?

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:09.749
There is speculation.

00:06:09.749 --> 00:06:14.379
You know, both sides love to say the other
side is really the violent one.

00:06:14.379 --> 00:06:18.819
I believe that when you look at political
violence, the groups that study this have

00:06:18.819 --> 00:06:23.689
found that it really is disproportionately
from the right against the left.

00:06:23.689 --> 00:06:29.879
But, you know, there's been speculation about
yesterday's milestone, the certification,

00:06:29.879 --> 00:06:37.630
the voting of the electors could trigger violence
or January 6th or January 20th, these different

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:39.229
dates.

00:06:39.229 --> 00:06:43.310
What what is your sense about what we might
expect and what might really lead to such

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:44.310
violence?

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:47.949
Yeah, I mean, my tendency I'm sort of an empiricist.

00:06:47.949 --> 00:06:48.949
I'm a reporter.

00:06:48.949 --> 00:06:54.860
I go places and see things and work from there
rather than generalizing trends and then making

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:56.300
predictions from there.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:07:01.759
So I'm guessing that going forward, we're
more likely to see things that are totally

00:07:01.759 --> 00:07:09.430
unforeseen come up rather than then things
like Republican stab in the back kind of narratives,

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:15.440
for example, being the ones that that that
define the the unrest in the year to come.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:21.620
So what I'm most worried about is various
unforeseen things that could blossom into

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:22.620
much, much more.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:26.430
So you take, for example, something like George
Floyd.

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:31.509
There's plenty of examples of police brutality,
of racism, that particular one.

00:07:31.509 --> 00:07:36.330
I don't think any of us could have predicted
that it would spark what it did.

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:39.009
And incidents like that will happen more and
more.

00:07:39.009 --> 00:07:44.770
It's never been easier to take one incident
that may very well be illustrative or emblematic

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:49.580
or in a good example of a larger trend and
then turn them into something that you could

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:52.050
never have predicted what they would become.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:08:00.460
So whenever there is any kind of armed protest,
whenever there is a face off between militias,

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:05.400
they in an American city, which seems to happen
as a matter of course, whenever there's any

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:09.509
kind of public demonstration, you have the
possibility where there will be a gun battle

00:08:09.509 --> 00:08:13.289
in America, in an American city, that could
mean dozens of people killed.

00:08:13.289 --> 00:08:14.810
I hope that doesn't happen.

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:15.960
But where does that start?

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:17.680
It starts with one gunshot.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:23.439
And I'm worried that there might be something
that that that begins in that totally unpredictable

00:08:23.439 --> 00:08:27.309
and even almost random way.

00:08:27.309 --> 00:08:31.680
And the effects of that could be it could
be rippling enormous.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:32.680
Yeah.

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:39.120
What you're really talking about is the importance
of what Nassim Nicholas Taleb calls black

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.120
swans.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:41.120
Right.

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:43.789
These rare events, which by their nature are
unpredictable risk models don't account for

00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:44.789
them.

00:08:44.789 --> 00:08:47.370
Risk models that say we are accounting for
really rare events.

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:52.560
Don't Talab kind of elaborate on this further
in his book Fooled by Randomness, it even

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:58.500
takes an important, important role in his
book Antifragile.

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:03.899
These are these are the fundamentally difficult
to to predict events and that's what makes

00:09:03.899 --> 00:09:05.680
it so difficult to answer these questions.

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:07.300
Yeah, that's right.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:13.550
These are spontaneous events and they are
extremely contingent on, you know, if it's

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:19.601
single gunshot that erupts, that that gun
battle that I hypothesized before, then it

00:09:19.601 --> 00:09:21.730
could be because that person leaned on his
gun the wrong way.

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:26.370
It could be that he he didn't have a good
breakfast that morning.

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:33.770
That kind of thing is usually what causes
to causes events to be to be kicked into motion.

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:38.730
I mean, if we were to look back a year ago,
there are some things we could have predicted,

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:39.730
some things we couldn't have.

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:42.660
We could have predicted that the president
of the United States would display all the

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:46.170
traits that he's shown and the rest of his
political life.

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:47.220
Those things.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:48.220
Yes.

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:54.779
But the very fact that we saw this failed
pressure test of of the regulatory and other

00:09:54.779 --> 00:10:00.410
apparatus of the United States to the pandemic,
probably not something that anybody had on

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:01.410
the bingo card.

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:08.620
So that kind of thing, I think is much more
likely to define 2021 than anything that that

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:10.000
could be easily foreseen.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:17.110
I have this idea that I'm curious to get your
thoughts on about there are competing forces

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:23.720
that sort of make it less likely that you
will see mass rallies, huge protests in the

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:29.029
United States to the level that they sort
of start shutting down aspects of the economy

00:10:29.029 --> 00:10:30.350
or cities or whatever the.

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:36.740
So maybe and it's on the one hand, you have
the combination of ready access to credit

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:42.490
and cheap stuff from China and other countries,
which keeps a lot of people just comfortable

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:46.910
enough not to go out and protest because they'd
rather stay home and watch the game, so to

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:47.910
speak.

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:50.440
And this is not to diminish civic activism.

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:52.800
It's not to pretend that everybody's in a
comfortable position.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:57.800
But there's there's a swath of the population
that may be in a different economy without

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:01.769
ready access to cheap, cheap stuff from China
and without ready access to credit with which

00:11:01.769 --> 00:11:06.310
to buy that stuff might be less comfortable
and thus more eager to go out and protest.

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:12.399
Now, on the other hand, you have this kind
of forced precarity, people who recognize,

00:11:12.399 --> 00:11:16.950
wait, if I take a day off to go protest, I
could lose my job or at least not get paid

00:11:16.950 --> 00:11:18.089
that day.

00:11:18.089 --> 00:11:21.529
And then my health insurance is in question
and then I don't know if I have food for my

00:11:21.529 --> 00:11:22.529
kids.

00:11:22.529 --> 00:11:26.320
And so that this incentivizes going out to
protest.

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:30.449
What do you think of that as a systemic analysis
that maybe so.

00:11:30.449 --> 00:11:36.470
I mean, the Russian Revolution famously might
not have happened if the weather hadn't permitted

00:11:36.470 --> 00:11:40.529
certain, you know, crossing of the rivers
and by by large groups of people.

00:11:40.529 --> 00:11:46.970
So it's not it's not impossible that there
would be would be things like that that would

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:55.779
be equally determinative of whether people
are willing to go out and and be the political

00:11:55.779 --> 00:11:57.480
en mass.

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:04.079
There's also, though, again, the relationship,
for example, between the protests that happened

00:12:04.079 --> 00:12:13.829
this summer and the pandemic, I think is still
misunderstood, underexploited.

00:12:13.829 --> 00:12:18.000
They seem in some ways to have nothing to
do with each other, police violence is something

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:19.829
that's happened for a very long time.

00:12:19.829 --> 00:12:26.240
The pandemic was it was almost like a short
break that people took from the pandemic to

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:28.800
deal with this urgent matter.

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:33.069
Now, I think there's probably more to it than
that.

00:12:33.069 --> 00:12:39.920
The effects on mass psychology, individual
psychology of being pent up, of being prevented

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:44.930
from seeing the faces of other human beings
is immense.

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:49.029
And once that stops, I think a lot of things
will actually change.

00:12:49.029 --> 00:12:54.829
They might not change in predictable ways,
but things like the politics of mass gatherings,

00:12:54.829 --> 00:12:58.470
that could be one of the things that that.

00:12:58.470 --> 00:13:06.230
Turns out to change a lot as a result of a
coming of save vaccine, in terms of the psychological,

00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:11.850
I've been thinking a lot about post covid
I think that even when mathematically things

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:17.470
go back to what they were before, where during
flu season, you have a higher risk of catching

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:19.230
the flu.

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:23.570
And it's less deadly than coronavirus, but
it's the same as before coronavirus.

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:28.589
I think that there will be risks that were
always there that people weren't maybe thinking

00:13:28.589 --> 00:13:29.589
of.

00:13:29.589 --> 00:13:34.880
And you may see folks, particularly in certain
risk categories, who just during flu season

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:39.230
might want to wear a mask or might stay home,
or I think there's going to be sort of a realignment

00:13:39.230 --> 00:13:44.610
as a flexible as much as humans return to
the mean and go back to the way things were.

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:48.620
I think that at least for four years, they're
going to be aspects of this with us.

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:54.389
Yeah, I mean, there's some just factual knowledge
that that we have that either didn't know

00:13:54.389 --> 00:13:59.050
before or we didn't care about before, like
the fact that that, you know, during flu season

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:03.790
you can possibly save thousands and thousands
of lives by doing something like wearing a

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:04.790
mask.

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:05.980
Every Japanese person do that.

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:07.100
Almost no Americans do that.

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:09.889
So that knowledge is with us for good.

00:14:09.889 --> 00:14:16.600
What I'm thinking of more, ah, is I'm thinking
more about different types of psychological

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:17.600
effects.

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:26.470
I mean, I've had a strange pandemic in that
I was I was isolated as we were all told to

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:27.579
be for four months at a time.

00:14:27.579 --> 00:14:29.709
I'm glad I did that.

00:14:29.709 --> 00:14:35.060
And then I've gone out and I've reported to
so and I've moved from country to country

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:39.980
to I've been traveling more than most people
this year and have seen how the pandemic looks

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:42.009
from from place to place.

00:14:42.009 --> 00:14:48.680
And I would not have guessed from my position
of solitude in those first months how different

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:50.720
the feeling in psychology was.

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:58.040
Mass psychology was of going out and experiencing
places where the pandemic hadn't hit as hard.

00:14:58.040 --> 00:15:04.439
So I think we can be lulled into thinking
that that that nothing deeply has changed

00:15:04.439 --> 00:15:08.879
about how we're looking at the world because
it changes slowly.

00:15:08.879 --> 00:15:15.310
And then once there is the great pandemic
jailbreak, because the curve has been broken

00:15:15.310 --> 00:15:17.410
or the vaccine has been distributed.

00:15:17.410 --> 00:15:23.810
And I think what will emerge and we will discover
with great surprise how different the world

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:26.860
has become while we were locked away.

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:29.899
That's that's what I'm actually mostly looking
forward to.

00:15:29.899 --> 00:15:34.779
I think the world will be a we'll discover
that the world suddenly improves once we are

00:15:34.779 --> 00:15:36.069
able to go out.

00:15:36.069 --> 00:15:43.060
But the fact of change, I have little doubt
about massive change.

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:44.060
Yes, absolutely.

00:15:44.060 --> 00:15:45.060
I absolutely agree.

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:49.540
We've been speaking with Graham Wood, a journalist
who writes for The Atlantic and is also author

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:52.720
of the book The Way of the Strangers.

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:53.980
So appreciate your time today, Graham.

00:15:53.980 --> 00:15:54.980
It was a pleasure.

00:15:54.980 --> 00:15:55.260
The David Pakman Show at David Pakman Dotcom.

